  For the runner I'd use Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m. It runs ES modules natively with nothing installed, which keeps the
  "clone it and open it" property intact — a test suite that needs a hundred packages to check a pure function is a bad
  trade at this size.

  [1mOne genuine tension to name:[0m the parts of this feature most likely to break in real use — permission prompts, tab
  visibility routing, persistence across reload — are exactly the parts unit tests can't reach. Playwright can drive all
  three (it can grant and deny notification permission directly). It's the heaviest thing on the list, and it's also
  the only thing that would actually catch a broken opt-in flow.

  Since this repo has no tooling configured at all, now is the cheapest moment to decide what to set up.

